TEST 28128 -- L3 traffic on guest vlan (IPv6) - Hw forwarding disabled
===========================================================================
Run:    2026-09-24 (device clock 2026-09-23 ~21:40-22:05 UTC), tb470
Build:  awplus_main-20260923-20
DUT:    IE520 3-member VCStack "IE520-stk" (members 1/3/4)
Setup:  two supplicant ports on stack member 3 -- port3.0.9 (tb470 eth3, sender) and
        port3.0.13 (tb470 eth1, receiver). eth2 (vlan1, behind IE520-sa) also captured.
        Guest VLAN config on both ports:
            vlan database / vlan 200 state enable
            aaa authentication dot1x default group radius   (no server: nobody authenticates)
            interface port3.0.9,port3.0.13 / dot1x port-control auto / auth guest-vlan 200
        `show auth supplicant`: both ports dot1x, "No supplicants"; `show vlan 200`: both (u).
        hw-forwarding ON adds `auth guest-vlan hw-forwarding` on both ports.
Traffic: from eth3, 50 pps: 500 frames to eth1's MAC ("known"), then 100 frames to an
        unlearned MAC 02:00:00:00:99:99 ("unknown"). One frame from each host first so
        the DUT learns both MACs in the port's current VLAN.
CPU path: stack member 3 `show platform counter sdma` -- RX/TX packet counter -- read
        just before and just after each send. Background CPU traffic is ~800-950 packets
        per 14 s, so the evidence is the like-for-like comparison: the SAME traffic with
        hw-forwarding off vs on. The difference is the frames the CPU handled.
Rate:   the loss-vs-line-rate half of the case needs a line-rate generator (ixia).
        This bench sends at 50 pps, so that half is UNMEASURED throughout.
Step 2: the case gives step 2 the same description as step 1 and expects "completely
        blocked". Interpreted, from the folder title "Suppress unicast flooding on Guest
        VLAN", as UNKNOWN unicast between supplicants. That is an interpretation.

BASELINE (feature OFF, both ports plain vlan1)
      IPv4 known 500 -> eth1 500, eth2 0       IPv4 unknown 100 -> eth1 100, eth2 100 (flood)
      IPv6 known 500 -> eth1 500, eth2 0       IPv6 unknown 100 -> eth1 100, eth2 100 (flood)
IPv6:   src 2001:db8:200::65 -> dst 2001:db8:200::1 (both in 2001:db8:200::/64).

VERDICT: FAIL -- with hw-forwarding disabled, IPv6 between supplicants is COMPLETELY
         BLOCKED unless the guest VLAN has an IPv6 address in the traffic's own subnet.
         The case itself defines the three variants below (guest VLAN with no IP address /
         same subnet / different subnet) and expects EVERY one of them to forward via the
         CPU ("loss ... bottleneck by cpu throughput ... verify traffic going via cpu").
         Variants 1 and 3 deliver nothing and the CPU counters do not rise: the traffic
         is dropped, not CPU-forwarded.

      variant (vlan200 IPv6 address)             known 500 -> eth1   unknown 100 -> eth1   member 3 sdma tx
      1  none                                        0/500               0/100             1116
      2  2001:db8:200::10/64  (same subnet)        500/500             100/100             1695
      3  2001:db8:300::10/64  (different subnet)     0/500               0/100             1130
      (eth2 received 0 in every variant)

  Variant 2 is CPU-forwarded: its tx is ~580 above variants 1 and 3, which matches the
  600 frames. Variants 1 and 3 forward nothing at all, so no loss rate can be read: the
  traffic is not delivered at any rate. For comparison, the IPv4 run on the same ports
  and in the same state (28126) forwarded 500/500 with no guest-VLAN address.
  With hw-forwarding ON (28129) all three variants forward 500/500.

Cleanup: as 28126 (same run); vlan200's IPv6 address removed first.
